Lemma Design System
1. Visual Character

Lemma should feel:

scholarly
mathematical
elegant
warm
editorial
contemporary
restrained

It should NOT feel:

like a generic SaaS dashboard
like a social-media platform
like a university administrative website
like a medieval/fantasy website
excessively futuristic
excessively minimalist to the point of sterility

The leather theme is primarily a colour and material reference, not an instruction to use literal leather textures.

2. Colour
Light Mode
Parchment       #F5EDE0
Dark Chocolate  #2B1509
Brown           #4A2410
Saddle          #8C4A1E
Brass           #B8894A
Tan             #C9A876

Use parchment as the dominant background.

Use dark chocolate for primary text.

Use saddle and brass selectively for interactive elements and emphasis.

Do not turn the entire interface brown.

Whitespace and parchment should dominate the visual composition.

3. Dark Mode

Dark mode should feel like the same physical material under different lighting.

Prefer:

very dark brown/chocolate background
parchment primary text
tan secondary text
brass/saddle accents

Avoid pure black unless required for a specific component.

Avoid unrelated cool colour palettes.

4. Typography

Use an editorial serif typeface for:

Lemma wordmark where appropriate
large headings
article titles
major editorial elements

Use a highly readable typeface for long-form article text.

Use a clean sans-serif for:

navigation
filters
buttons
metadata
interface controls

Mathematical typography must be given sufficient size, spacing, and contrast.

5. Homepage

The first viewport should be visually simple.

The visitor should primarily see:

logo/name
motto
atmospheric background

The visual experience should gradually reveal the publication.

Use subtle transitions such as:

opacity
translation
scale
background movement
gradual emergence of content

Do not use excessive motion.

Respect:

prefers-reduced-motion

6. Navigation Drawer

The drawer should feel like part of the publication rather than a generic application sidebar.

It should contain:

Lemma logo
navigation
search
author dashboard
appearance control

The drawer must work equally well on desktop and mobile.

On mobile, it may become a near-full-screen navigation surface.

7. Cards

Article cards should be editorial rather than generic rounded SaaS cards.

Avoid excessive:

rounded corners
shadows
gradients
badges
decorative borders

Cards should prioritize:

title
topic
author
date
short description
peer-review status where relevant
8. Article Pages

Article pages should be calmer than the homepage.

Prioritize:

readable line length
generous whitespace
clear hierarchy
excellent equation rendering
high-quality image presentation
clear captions
accessible code blocks

Do not use distracting animation while reading.

9. Accessibility

Maintain:

sufficient colour contrast
keyboard navigation
visible focus states
semantic headings
accessible buttons
alt text for images
reduced-motion support
responsive typography

Aesthetic considerations must not override accessibility.

10. Responsive Design

Do not simply shrink desktop layouts.

Design explicitly for:

desktop
laptop
tablet
mobile

Article pages, search filters, navigation, code blocks, equations, and images must each be tested at small widths.

11. Logo

Use the supplied logo reference as the visual source of truth.

The logo should be implemented as a reusable component.

Preferred location:

src/components/brand/LemmaLogo.tsx

Do not duplicate raw SVG markup throughout the application.

If the logo can safely support light/dark adaptation without changing its identity, use the design tokens.

Otherwise preserve its original colours.